From 19fddc208348ca6faaa2ce199bc49abe48e9f9b9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: omesh <roland@nordhain.com>
Date: Sun, 19 Apr 2026 08:36:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 11/41] grpc: BlockStream service implementation with extensive
 PERF logging
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 1a step 2 — the actual gRPC service. No server spawn yet (commit 3),
but the service is fully implemented and `cargo check -p cuprated` passes.

Service design (binaries/cuprated/src/rpc/grpc.rs):
- BlockStreamService wraps CupratedRpcHandler so the streaming endpoint
  feeds from the same blockchain/txpool services as the bin RPC. TX
  pruning, output-index batching and DB readers are all reused via
  bin_handlers::capped_block_complete_entries + output_indices_for_blocks.
  Visibility of those helpers raised from private to pub(crate) so the
  grpc module can call them — no logic duplicated.
- Each chunk's payload is the same epee-serialized GetBlocksResponse the
  bin RPC produces (with pool-info fields zeroed out). The wallet decodes
  it with its existing COMMAND_RPC_GET_BLOCKS_FAST::response parser, so
  no second deserializer is needed on the wallet side. The protobuf layer
  is purely the HTTP/2 envelope — that is where all of the network-layer
  wins come from. (See proto file header for full rationale.)
- Bounded mpsc(2) between producer task and HTTP/2 send loop. Small on
  purpose: a full buffer means the client is not draining, and we want
  that to surface as backpressure (visible as send_ms / bp_ratio in the
  PERF log) instead of buffering chunks in memory. HTTP/2 flow control
  picks up the slack naturally.
- DEFAULT_CHUNK_BLOCKS = 200. Hint-clampable by client between 16 and
  2000. 200 keeps individual chunk encode cost in the few-tens-of-ms range
  so flow control responds before the pipeline stalls.

PERF logging (per Roland's feedback — bake heavy logging in from day 1):
- OPEN: id, client_req_id, start, stop, prune, no_miner_tx, chunk_blocks,
  active_streams (atomic), open_epoch_ms (wall clock for cross-machine).
- CHUNK (per chunk): id, seq, start, n_blocks, payload_bytes, db_ms,
  idx_ms, enc_ms, send_ms, bp_ratio (send_ms / upstream_ms — backpressure
  indicator), cum_ms, cum_bytes, cum_mbs (running average throughput),
  chain_tip.
- BACKPRESSURE: dedicated line when bp_ratio > 2 so it's grep-friendly.
- CLOSE: reason (tip_reached / client_disconnect / zero_blocks /
  encode_err), totals, avg_mbs.
- CLOSE_ERR: error path with full debug print.

Server-side request ids are auto-generated when the client doesn't supply
one, but the wallet should always send `client_request_id` so log lines
on both sides correlate without a shared clock — same pattern as the
X-Perf-Req-Id header on bin RPC.

The auto-generated tonic code triggers the workspace's strict
unnecessary_qualifications + clippy::pedantic lints; suppressed at the
proto module boundary only — our hand-written code remains under the
full lint set.
